# 5. Counters

Counters are sequential circuits that change their stored binary state in a defined sequence.

## Asynchronous (Ripple) Counters

In an asynchronous counter, the clock is applied directly to the first flip-flop and the following flip-flops are triggered by the previous stage.

### Practiced

- 4-bit Asynchronous Up Counter
- 4-bit Asynchronous Down Counter

🔗 [4-bit Async Up Counter](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/KFJK)

🔗 [4-bit Async Down Counter](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/YnrQ)

---

## Synchronous Counters

In a synchronous counter, all flip-flops receive the **same clock** and state transitions occur together.

### Practiced

- 4-bit Synchronous Up Counter
- 4-bit Synchronous Down Counter

🔗 [4-bit Sync Up Counter](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/j4dj)

🔗 [4-bit Sync Down Counter](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/jbR8)

---

## Asynchronous vs Synchronous Counter

| Feature | Asynchronous Counter | Synchronous Counter |
|---|---|---|
| Clocking | Ripple from stage to stage | Common clock |
| State Transition | Propagates through stages | Happens together |
| Timing | Ripple / propagation delay | More coordinated |
| Design | Simpler structure | More controlled timing |

### Practical Relevance

Counters are important building blocks in:

- Timers
- Event counters
- Frequency division
- Digital control
- RTL systems

