# 6. Finite State Machine (FSM) Basics

An **FSM (Finite State Machine)** represents a sequential system using a finite number of states.

### FSM Design Flow

**Inputs → Current State → Next-State Logic → Next State → Output**

Two important FSM styles were practiced.

---

## Moore FSM

Output depends only on the **current state**.

**Output = f(Current State)**

🔗 [Moore FSM — RTL + Testbench + Simulation](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/bi62)

---

## Mealy FSM

Output depends on the **current state and input**.

**Output = f(Current State, Input)**

🔗 [Mealy FSM — RTL + Testbench + Simulation](https://www.edaplayground.com/x/BMyD)

---

## Moore vs Mealy

| Feature | Moore FSM | Mealy FSM |
|---|---|---|
| Output depends on | Current State | Current State + Input |
| Output change | State dependent | State + input dependent |
| Main focus | State-based output | Input + state based output |

### Practical Relevance

FSMs are widely used in:

- Control logic
- Protocol controllers
- Sequence detectors
- Communication logic
- Digital system control

